Currently trying to use the example date format to trigger an error message on my local environment with a running REST API, and not getting errors on parse... Was something changed that affects this since the issue was opened?

```
$body = @{
    limit = 10
    filter = @{
        must = @(
            @{
                key = "updated"
                range = @{
                    gt = "2014-01-01T00:00:00"
                }
            }
        )
    }
} | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 10

Invoke-RestMethod -Method Post -Uri "http://localhost:6333/collections/test_collection/points/scroll" -Body $body -ContentType "application/json"
```

This is the powershell request I'm sending in, and simply getting an OK response on DateTimePayloadType::from_str(). Manual debug printing shows

```
Attempting to parse datetime: '2014-01-01T00:00:00'
Successfully parsed: DateTimeWrapper(2014-01-01T00:00:00Z)
```

EDIT: I think I answered my own question. I'll continue to sniff out valid date formats that still _don't_ have handling to test the improved error messaging
